<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Finding when the amount of money in the bank account is increasing</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Suppose that the amount of money in a bank account is given by</p>
<p>\( \qquad P (t) = {@pt@}\) </p><p>where \(t\) is in years. During the first \({@time@}\) years in which the account is open when is the amount of money in the account increasing?</p>
<p>Find the increasing values in the \([{@tpi@}, {@time@}]\) interval:(Give your answer in exact form or correct to \(4\)dp where necessary)</p>
<p>For the interval  \([{@tpi@}, {@2*tpi@}]\) :</p>
<p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>For the interval  \([{@3*tpi@}, {@time@}]\) :</p>
<p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>


<p>To find when the amount of money in the account is increasing, we need to find when the derivative of the function \(P(t)\) is positive.</p>
<p>The derivative of \(P(t)\) is: \(P'(t) = {@dpt@}\)</p>


<p>Now, we need to find when \(P'(t) > 0\) over the first {@time@} years, which corresponds to the interval \(0 \leq t \leq {@time@} \). We'll analyze the sign of \(P'(t)\) in this interval.</p>

<p>Let's start by finding when \(P'(t) = 0\):</p>
<p>\(
\displaystyle
\begin{align}
{@dpt@} &= 0 \\
{@coeff(dpt,sin(t),1)@}\sin(t) &= {@-coeff(dpt,cos(t),1)@}\cos(t) \\
   {@(lhs_sol/rhs_sol)*coef1@} &= {@coef1@} \\
    \tan(t) &= {@coef1@}
\end{align}
\)</p>
<br> To find solutions for \(t\), we consider that the tangent function is periodic with a period of {@tpi@}. So,  
<p>\(
\displaystyle
\begin{align}
t &= {@decimalplaces(t1,4)@}+2\pi n, \quad n=0,\pm1,\pm2,...\\
 & and  \\
t &= {@decimalplaces(t2,4)@} +2\pi n, \quad n=0,\pm1,\pm2,...\\
\end{align}
\)</p>

<p>\(
\displaystyle
\begin{align}
t_1 &= {@t1@} \approx {@decimalplaces(t1,4)@}\\
t_2 &={@t2@} \approx {@decimalplaces(t2,4)@} \\
t_3 &={@t3@} \approx  {@decimalplaces(t3,4)@} \\
t_4 &= {@time@} 
\end{align}
\)</p>

<p>So, it looks like the amount of money in the bank account will be increasing during the
following intervals:</p>

<p>\[{@decimalplaces(t1,4)@} < t < {@decimalplaces(t2,4)@} \quad \text{and} \quad {@decimalplaces(t3,4)@} < t < {@time@} \]</p>

<p>Note that we can’t say anything about what is happening after \(t = {@time@}\) since we haven’t done any work for \(t\)’s after that point.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
